.TH LAZY 1 "August 2026" lazy "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lazy \- frecency-based smart cd and editor launcher
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lazy
.br
.B cd
.RI [ directory-fragment ]
.br
.B $EDITOR
.RI [ file-fragment ]
.SH AVAILABILITY
bash, zsh (Tab completion is Bash-only)
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBlazy\fR is a heavily stripped-down, zero-bloat fork of \fBz\fR. It tracks your most-used directories and files by 'frecency' (frequency + recency) so you can jump to them by typing a fragment of the name instead of the full path.

This fork extends the exact same concept to \fBfiles\fR, but removes all the clunky manual CLI commands. It is designed to be a completely silent, drop-in background utility that makes your \fBcd\fR and your \fB$EDITOR\fR context-aware and highly efficient.

When you type \fBcd foo\fR, and \fBfoo\fR is not in the current directory, it fuzzy-searches its database and jumps to the best match.

When you type \fB$EDITOR foo\fR (e.g., \fBnvim foo\fR), it fuzzy-searches its file database. It intelligently checks if you have write access to the target file. If you don't, it instantly recognizes the boundary and automatically executes \fBsudo\fR or \fBsudo -e\fR instead.

If you pass any standard editor flags (e.g., \fB-v\fR, \fB+42\fR), the wrapper instantly downgrades into a raw passthrough, bypassing the fuzzy database and safely passing arguments directly to the editor.
.SH OPTIONS
\fBlazy\fR does not use standard CLI options. It is invoked via wrappers around \fBcd\fR and your configured \fBEDITOR\fR. Running \fBlazy\fR with no arguments prints the current configuration and stats.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
You can override these by exporting them before sourcing \fBlazy\fR in your \fB.bashrc\fR or \fB.zshrc\fR:
.TP
.B _LAZY_DIR_DB
directory datafile path (default: ~/.lazydir)
.TP
.B _LAZY_FILE_DB
file datafile path (default: ~/.lazyfile)
.TP
.B _LAZY_HALF_LIFE
number of commands before a score halves (default: 85)
.TP
.B _LAZY_MAX_ENTRIES
maximum number of entries to track per file (default: 1000)
.TP
.B _LAZY_DIR_BLACKLIST
array of path prefixes to ignore for cd (default: empty)
.TP
.B _LAZY_FILE_BLACKLIST
array of path prefixes to ignore for editor (default: empty)
.TP
.B EDITOR
program used to open matched files (default: $VISUAL, else nano)
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.lazydir
Default data file for directory frecency.
.TP
.I ~/.lazyfile
Default data file for file frecency.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B cd foo
Safely enters local directory 'foo' if it exists, otherwise cd to most frecent dir matching 'foo'.
.TP
.B nvim foo
Fuzzy-searches ~/.lazyfile for 'foo', resolves the absolute path, and opens it.
.TP
.B nvim /etc/hosts
Detects lack of permissions, and securely opens using \fBsudo -e\fR.
.TP
.B nvim +42 foo.txt
Bypasses tracking and passes arguments directly to the editor.
.SH AUTHOR
Original \fBz\fR core frecency concept by rupa deadwyler.
.br
Exponential decay and event-clock architecture by Joerg van den Hoff (jghub).
.br
File tracking, smart wrappers & auto-escalation by elseawhy.
.SH SEE ALSO
bash(1), zsh(1), sudo(8)
